                <pb facs="1248" n="1144" sameAs="frus:frus1913/pg1144"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 823.00/107.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Minister to the
                            Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegrams.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Lima</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1913-08-05">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">August 5, 1913</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your August 4. The general opinion, corroborated by Billinghurst supporters, is
                    that the ex-President will soon be unconditionally released. His son and brother
                    are now at liberty. Representations have been made at the Foreign Office and
                    among the Diplomatic Corps for concerted action. Application for an audience
                    with the President remains unanswered, but an early interview was promised
                    yesterday at the Foreign Office.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Howard</hi>.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
